adjudication.rationale:
The new helper `parseRefreshTokenResponse` explicitly injects a literal placeholder when the parsed token response lacks a refresh token:

- `parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`:
  ```ts
  if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
    refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
  }
  ```

In `office365calendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`, the refreshed token payload is merged into the existing stored credentials:
```ts
const tokenResponse = parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseJson, refreshTokenResponseSchema);
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update(...)
```
If Microsoft’s refresh response omits `refresh_token` (common), `parseRefreshTokenResponse` will set it to the literal string, and the object spread will overwrite the previously stored real `refresh_token` before persisting via `prisma.credential.update`. That silently destroys the real refresh token for that credential, making future refresh impossible if the system later relies on the stored refresh token.
